  <p>Is it realistic to think a couple can rebuild after this? The research is more hopeful than you might expect. In <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15709841/">a study of couples in marital therapy</a>, the 19 couples who had disclosed an affair started out more distressed than the rest and improved more steeply, ending treatment at about the same place as the other distressed couples (Atkins et al., 2005, <em>Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology</em>). It is a small group inside one trial, so hold it loosely. That is not a promise about your particular relationship, and it is not a reason to rush. It just means the door is not closed, even when it feels like it is.</p>
  <p>When a couple wants to move faster or go deeper than weekly sessions allow, a Couples Intensive, a focused four-to-eight-hour block in a single day, can do a lot of work at once. It's one of the ways this practice makes room for the harder cases. <a href="/couples-intensive">See how a Couples Intensive works</a>, or ask about it on your free call.</p>
